---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Photo from '../../components/Photo.astro';
import { site, allTowns } from '../../data/site';
import { services } from '../../data/services';
import { photos } from '../../data/photos';
import { reviews } from '../../data/reviews';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return services.map((service) => ({ params: { slug: service.slug }, props: { service } }));
}

const { service } = Astro.props;
const photo = photos.find((item) => item.id === service.photoId) ?? photos[0];
const others = services.filter((item) => item.slug !== service.slug);

const quote =
  service.slug === 'kitchen-spraying'
    ? reviews[0]
    : service.slug === 'woodwork-and-stairs'
      ? reviews[3]
      : service.slug === 'wallpapering'
        ? reviews[3]
        : reviews[1];

const crumbs = {
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: site.url },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Services', item: `${site.url}/services` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: service.title, item: `${site.url}/services/${service.slug}` },
  ],
};

const serviceSchema = {
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: service.title,
  serviceType: service.title,
  description: `${service.summary} ${service.description}`,
  provider: { '@id': `${site.url}/#business` },
  areaServed: allTowns.slice(0, 8).map((name) => ({ '@type': 'City', name })),
  url: `${site.url}/services/${service.slug}`,
};
---

<Base
  title={service.title}
  description={`${service.title} in Sandy and nearby towns. ${service.summary}`}
  image={`/work/${photo.id}.webp`}
  schemas={[crumbs, serviceSchema]}
>
  <Fragment slot="hero">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/services">Services</a> / {service.title}</p>
    <h1>{service.title}</h1>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="band prose">
    <Photo photo={photo} eager caption sizes="(min-width: 760px) 640px, 100vw" />
    <p>{service.description}</p>
    {service.slug === 'interior-painting' && (
      <>
        <p>
          A typical room is ceilings first, then walls, then the woodwork. New plaster needs
          a proper start, or the paint will not sit. Simon has painted newly plastered ceilings
          and walls for repeat customers, including Anita Philpot, who also had internal doors
          and wardrobes sprayed.
        </p>
        <p>
          He will talk through colour if you want a second view. Bring a chart, or ask him
          which finish will wear well in a hall or a kitchen.
        </p>
      </>
    )}
    {service.slug === 'kitchen-spraying' && (
      <>
        <p>
          The navy kitchen in the photo is the sort of result customers ask for: even colour,
          sharp edges, and the room put back together. Anita Philpot wrote that Simon sprayed
          her units a completely different colour, and that she would not use another painter.
        </p>
        <p>
          Spraying takes preparation. Doors come off, surfaces are keyed, and dust is kept
          down. That is slower than a quick brush coat, and it is why the finish looks flat.
        </p>
      </>
    )}
    {service.slug === 'woodwork-and-stairs' && (
      <>
        <p>
          Stairs are in use while the paint dries, so the order of work matters. Treads are
          covered. Spindles and handrails are coated so you do not feel brush drag on the rail.
          Dark colours, like the black woodwork in the photos, need enough coats to cover.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the old paint is cracking, it has to come off. The job photos show that stage:
          a scraper on the stringer, then the finished rail. Peel Stop is used when the
          remaining paint needs binding before the new coats.
        </p>
      </>
    )}
    {service.slug === 'wallpapering' && (
      <>
        <p>
          Rob Shirley asked Simon to redecorate a whole house after an extension. The job
          included new plaster, oak doors, and a lot of difficult wallpaper. He wrote that
          the finish was right in every room, and he called Simon back years later.
        </p>
        <p>
          Wallpaper and paint often share a room. Simon will say which walls should be papered
          and which should be painted, and he will line up the pattern so the joins do not
          jump out.
        </p>
      </>
    )}
    {service.slug === 'exterior-painting' && (
      <>
        <p>
          Outside paint fails when the timber is damp or the old coat is peeling. Simon checks
          that before he prices the job. Sheds, doors and fascias are the usual outside work.
          House walls in masonry are a different job, and he will tell you if it is not one
          he should take.
        </p>
        <p>
          The door in the photo is inside a house. Outside doors are painted with the same
          care, once the timber is dry.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Facebook page shows painted exterior timber, including a shed finished in a
          strong orange. Indoor photos are more common on Google, because that is most of the work.
        </p>
      </>
    )}
    <h2>Where this work is done</h2>
    <p>
      From Sandy, across Biggleswade, Potton, Bedford, St Neots and the other towns on the
      <a href="/areas">areas page</a>. If your town is not listed, phone and ask.
    </p>
    <figure class="quote">
      <blockquote>
        <p style="white-space: pre-line">{quote.text}</p>
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption>{quote.name}, five stars on Google</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p><a href="/contact">Ask for a quote</a></p>
    <h2>Other services</h2>
    <ul>
      {others.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`/services/${item.slug}`}>{item.title}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
